data.content:
## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

**Pattern identified:** Timeout enforcement + ThreadAbortException catch + ResetAbort

Your code uses `Thread.Abort()` to kill a thread that exceeds a timeout. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`. The fix is cooperative cancellation via `CancellationToken`.

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);

        try
        {
            var task = Task.Run(work, cts.Token);
            task.Wait(cts.Token);
            return task.Result;
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### If `work` is long-running and must respond to cancellation

The above only prevents *starting* work after timeout. If `work` is already running, it won't be interrupted — **cooperative cancellation requires the work to check the token**. Expose the token:

```csharp
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;

    try
    {
        var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);
        task.Wait(token);
        return task.Result;
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
    catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
    {
        throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
    }
}

// Caller usage:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    foreach (var item in items)
    {
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested(); // checkpoint
        ProcessItem(item);
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(30));
```

### What changed and why

| Removed | Replacement | Reason |
|---------|-------------|--------|
| `new Thread(...)` | `Task.Run(...)` | Uses thread pool; avoids manual thread management |
| `thread.Abort()` | `CancellationTokenSource(timeout)` | Cooperative cancellation — `.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` in .NET 6+ |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | `catch (OperationCanceledException)` | New exception type for cancellation |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Removed entirely | No equivalent needed; cancellation doesn't auto-rethrow |
| `thread.Join(timeout)` | `task.Wait(token)` | Token-driven wait instead of manual timeout check |

### Key Pitfalls

1. **Cancellation is not immediate.** Unlike `Thread.Abort()` which injected an exception at any point, cooperative cancellation only fires at `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` checkpoints. If `work` does a long blocking call (e.g., `Thread.Sleep`, synchronous I/O), it won't stop until that call completes. Replace `Thread.Sleep(ms)` with `token.WaitHandle.WaitOne(ms)`.

2. **Dispose `CancellationTokenSource`.** It holds timers internally. The `using` statement handles this.

3. **Truly uncooperative code can't be cancelled.** If `work` calls third-party code you can't modify to accept a token, the only reliable option is running it in a **separate process** and using `Process.Kill()` on timeout.

4. **Don't swallow `OperationCanceledException`.** The `when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)` filter ensures you only catch timeout-caused cancellation, not unrelated cancellation from an outer scope.
